1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,938
Good morning. My name's Lucy Crittenden,

2
00:00:03,938 --> 00:00:12,798
and I'm the Director of Operations for an organisation that arranges volunteering in this part of the country.

3
00:00:12,798 --> 00:00:19,690
I'm hoping I can persuade one or two of you to become volunteers yourselves.

4
00:00:19,690 --> 00:00:24,740
Let me start by briefly explaining what we mean by volunteering.

5
00:00:24,740 --> 00:00:29,740
Volunteers are teenagers and adults who choose to spend some time

6
00:00:29,740 --> 00:00:32,531
unpaid, helping other people in some way.

7
00:00:32,531 --> 00:00:37,140
Most volunteers devote two or three hours to this every week

8
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:39,890
while a few do much more.

9
00:00:39,890 --> 00:00:45,500
The people they help may have physical or behavioural difficulties, for example.

10
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:48,720
Volunteers can do all sorts of things

11
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:51,176
depending on their own abilities and interests.

12
00:00:51,176 --> 00:00:55,000
If they're supporting a family that's struggling

13
00:00:55,180 --> 00:00:58,879
for example, they may be able to give them tips on cooking,

14
00:00:58,879 --> 00:01:04,960
or recommend how to plan their budget or how to shop sensibly on their income.

15
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:08,110
They might even do some painting or wallpapering,

16
00:01:08,110 --> 00:01:13,228
perhaps alongside any members of the family who are able to do it.

17
00:01:13,228 --> 00:01:18,740
Or even do some babysitting so that parents can go out for a while.

18
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:23,601
The benefit from volunteering isn't only for the people being helped.

19
00:01:23,601 --> 00:01:25,811
Volunteers also gain from it:

20
00:01:25,811 --> 00:01:31,556
they're using their skills to cope with somebody's mental or physical ill health,

21
00:01:31,556 --> 00:01:36,760
and volunteering may be a valuable element of their CV

22
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:38,185
when they're applying for jobs:

23
00:01:38,185 --> 00:01:43,930
employers usually look favourably on someone who's given up time to help others.

24
00:01:43,930 --> 00:01:50,888
Significantly, most volunteers feel that what they're doing gives them a purpose in their lives.

25
00:01:50,888 --> 00:01:55,280
And in my opinion, they're lucky in that respect

26
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:58,310
as many people don't have that feeling.

27
00:01:58,310 --> 00:02:04,044
Now I'd like to tell you what some of our volunteers have said about what they do,

28
00:02:04,044 --> 00:02:09,440
to give you an idea of the range of ways in which they can help people.

29
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:14,627
Habib supports an elderly lady who's beginning to show signs of dementia.

30
00:02:14,627 --> 00:02:18,517
Once a week they, along with other elderly people,

31
00:02:18,517 --> 00:02:25,000
go to the local community centre, where a group of people come in and sing.

32
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,780
The songs take the listeners back to their youth,

33
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:34,660
and for a little while they can forget the difficulties that they face now.

34
00:02:34,660 --> 00:02:38,024
Our volunteer Consuela is an amazing woman.

35
00:02:38,024 --> 00:02:43,791
She has difficulty walking herself, but she doesn't let that stop her.

36
00:02:43,791 --> 00:02:47,420
She helps a couple of people with similar difficulties

37
00:02:47,420 --> 00:02:51,000
who had almost stopped walking altogether.

38
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:59,440
By using herself as an example, Consuela encourages them to walk more and more.

39
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:06,720
Minh visits a young man who lives alone and can't leave his home on his own

40
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:09,480
so he hardly ever saw anyone.

41
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:12,423
But together they go out to the cinema,

42
00:03:12,423 --> 00:03:18,310
or to see friends the young man hadn't been able to visit for a long time.

43
00:03:18,310 --> 00:03:22,281
Tanya visits an elderly woman once a week.

44
00:03:22,281 --> 00:03:29,230
When the woman found out that Tanya is a professional dressmaker, she got interested.

45
00:03:29,230 --> 00:03:37,690
Tanya showed her some soft toys she'd made, and the woman decided to try it herself.

46
00:03:37,690 --> 00:03:44,620
And now she really enjoys it, and spends hours making toys.

47
00:03:44,620 --> 00:03:49,820
They're not perhaps up to Tanya's standard yet

48
00:03:49,820 --> 00:03:52,660
but she gains a lot of pleasure from doing it.

49
00:03:52,660 --> 00:03:59,680
Alexei is a volunteer with a family that faces a number of difficulties.

50
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:03,768
By calmly talking over possible solutions with family members,

51
00:04:03,768 --> 00:04:07,855
he's helping them to realise that they aren't helpless,

52
00:04:07,855 --> 00:04:12,851
and that they can do something themselves to improve their situation.

53
00:04:12,851 --> 00:04:16,030
This has been great for their self-esteem.

54
00:04:16,030 --> 00:04:20,300
And the last volunteer I'll mention

55
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:23,099
though there are plenty more, is Juba. She volunteers

56
00:04:23,099 --> 00:04:27,260
with a teenage girl with learning difficulties

57
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:30,640
who wasn't very good at talking to other people.

58
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:33,907
Juba's worked very patiently with her,

59
00:04:33,907 --> 00:04:41,530
and now the girl is far better at expressing herself, and understanding other people.

60
00:04:41,530 --> 00:04:50,520
OK, I hope that's given you an idea of what volunteering is all about. Now I'd like...
